<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Cantabria</h1><p class="st-meta">574 CE – 739 CE (approx.) · capital Amaya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Visigothic frontier duchy in northern Spain whose last duke, Peter, was father of King Alfonso I of Asturias.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Leovigild</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a> in 574, taking its stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pena-amaya">Amaya</a>. A frontier duchy was then set up to watch the mountain peoples and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a>, though its extent and organisation are poorly documented.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy was ruled by a dux appointed by the Visigothic kings, with its centre probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pena-amaya">Amaya</a> in the north of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>. Its population lived from herding and farming in the valleys of the Cantabrian range, and Visigothic control seems to have been loose.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Muslim conquest of 711, Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-cantabria">Peter of Cantabria</a>, known only from the Asturian chronicles, probably kept some independence. His son Alfonso married the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagius-of-asturias">Pelagius</a> and became Alfonso I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> in 739, uniting the duchy with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-cantabria.jpg" alt="Duchy of Cantabria" width="320" height="200"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ducado_de_Cantabria.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>574 CE – 739 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amaya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Peter of Cantabria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ducatus Cantabriae</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=656&amp;focus=duchy-of-cantabria" aria-label="Duchy of Cantabria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4579 662 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Cantabria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4899 918L5035 888M4899 918L4859 895"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5035 888h0M4859 895h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4899 918h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4899 918h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Amaya. <a href="/atlas?year=656&amp;focus=duchy-of-cantabria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
